WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.723
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.723 --> 00:00:09.403
Hello and welcome everyone to the
Stanford MBA maximizing the classroom and

00:00:09.403 --> 00:00:11.330
academic experience panel.

00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:12.869
My name is Lisa John Angela and

00:00:12.869 --> 00:00:17.410
I'm a director of MBA admissions as well
as an alumni of the Stanford MBA program.

00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:21.167
And I'll be joined today by some of my
fellow alumni who will also share their

00:00:21.167 --> 00:00:24.867
experiences about what it's like both
inside and outside the classroom at

00:00:24.867 --> 00:00:27.820
the Stanford Graduate School
of Business or Stanford GSB.

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:33.181
Just to know what to expect for
today's session, really our main focus

00:00:33.181 --> 00:00:36.691
is to allow you an opportunity
to engage with our community and

00:00:36.691 --> 00:00:41.610
to really learn more about the academic
experience at the Stanford GSB.

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:45.388
We'll talk a little bit more about
the resources that are available to you to

00:00:45.388 --> 00:00:46.461
build your toolkit.

00:00:46.461 --> 00:00:48.982
You'll have an opportunity
to meet our panelists,

00:00:48.982 --> 00:00:51.113
I'll start us off with
a little bit of Q&amp;A.

00:00:51.113 --> 00:00:54.120
And then really we want to
address your questions.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:57.370
So at any point in today's session,
if you have a question,

00:00:57.370 --> 00:01:00.070
feel free to pop that into the Q&amp;A box.

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:02.247
And our panelists will
try to address that.

00:01:02.247 --> 00:01:06.165
If you see a question that someone else
has asked that you particularly like,

00:01:06.165 --> 00:01:09.796
feel free to click on that little
thumbs up symbol which will upvote it.

00:01:09.796 --> 00:01:13.878
And we'll try to answer as many questions
as we can in the approximate hour that we

00:01:13.878 --> 00:01:15.030
have together today.

00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:20.699
But let's talk a little bit about all
the resources that are available to you

00:01:20.699 --> 00:01:22.882
as you build your toolkit at the GSB,

00:01:22.882 --> 00:01:26.460
this is truly a transformative
educational experience.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:30.810
And one of the things I love about
the GSB is that we use whatever

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:35.170
pedagogy that really optimizes
your learning outcomes.

00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:38.572
So it's going to vary class by class and
even within a class,

00:01:38.572 --> 00:01:40.720
sometimes week by week.

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:45.265
You're going to be exposed to case
studies and project based courses,

00:01:45.265 --> 00:01:47.156
role plays, simulations.

00:01:47.156 --> 00:01:49.958
You might actually be
rolling up your sleeves and

00:01:49.958 --> 00:01:53.520
building a prototype in one of
your design oriented courses.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:56.562
You might be participating in
an action based learning program and

00:01:56.562 --> 00:01:59.200
partnering with different corporations.

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:03.018
Or you might be in some courses that
are maybe a bit more internal focused and

00:02:03.018 --> 00:02:06.548
you're really thinking about your
own personal leadership style.

00:02:06.548 --> 00:02:10.857
And getting a lot of really important
leadership coaching and mentoring.

00:02:10.857 --> 00:02:13.812
Your first year is predominantly
the core curriculum.

00:02:13.812 --> 00:02:16.920
We want to make sure that you're grounded
on all of those general management

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:18.160
disciplines.

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:21.906
But even during your core, you'll see some
different levels based on your experiences

00:02:21.906 --> 00:02:25.860
to date, and what you've studied,
and what you've been working in.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:29.835
Towards the end of your first year, you'll
start taking some elective courses and

00:02:29.835 --> 00:02:32.164
your second year is
entirely elective courses,

00:02:32.164 --> 00:02:35.531
so if you'd like to go deep in
a particular area, you absolutely can.

00:02:35.531 --> 00:02:38.518
But more often than not, we find our
students ramping up their skill sets and

00:02:38.518 --> 00:02:42.190
their knowledge in a few different
areas where they have interests.

00:02:42.190 --> 00:02:45.670
At the business school, there are a few
different research centers and

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:49.440
learning hubs that you can take advantage
of, so for example, our Center for

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:52.579
Social Innovation or our Center for
Entrepreneurial Studies.

00:02:52.579 --> 00:02:57.382
But one of the wonderful things I love
about Stanford GSB is that you're not just

00:02:57.382 --> 00:02:59.748
within that business school bubble but

00:02:59.748 --> 00:03:03.993
instead you are a part of that entire
Stanford University ecosystem.

00:03:03.993 --> 00:03:08.960
And you have access to all seven different
Stanford University graduate schools.

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:12.958
So Stanford is home to some of the top
programs in a number of different fields.

00:03:12.958 --> 00:03:17.506
Whether that's law, or medicine, or
education, or engineering, humanities, or

00:03:17.506 --> 00:03:21.310
sciences, there are all these
disciplines readily available to you and

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:22.950
we say take advantage of that.

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:27.525
Take classes across the street, as we say,
and approximately 80% of our MBA students

00:03:27.525 --> 00:03:31.020
do take classes in other
parts of Stanford University.

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:35.581
And these days about 20% of our MBA
students are actually pursuing a joint or

00:03:35.581 --> 00:03:38.840
dual degree during our
time in our MBA program.

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:41.771
So maybe they're doing
another master's program, or

00:03:41.771 --> 00:03:45.959
a law degree, or a medical degree, or
something else in addition to their MBA.

00:03:45.959 --> 00:03:49.523
And then throughout your time at Stanford,
you're not alone,

00:03:49.523 --> 00:03:53.880
there's this whole team of folks who
are there to support you on your journey.

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:58.346
And this ranges from academic, and
career, and student life advisors,

00:03:58.346 --> 00:04:01.454
leadership, fellows,
communication coaches.

00:04:01.454 --> 00:04:04.766
The faculty, obviously who are here
to help you and support you and

00:04:04.766 --> 00:04:09.975
a whole host of general health and mental
health services that are available to you.

00:04:09.975 --> 00:04:13.714
But let's hear directly from
some of our panelists and

00:04:13.714 --> 00:04:17.133
some of our alumni,
an opportunity to meet them.

00:04:17.133 --> 00:04:20.669
I'll have each of our panelists,
if you wouldn't mind,

00:04:20.669 --> 00:04:22.841
briefly introducing yourselves.

00:04:22.841 --> 00:04:25.530
We'll go in the order that we
see here on the screen, so

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:27.350
Diego if you'd like to start us off.

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:33.088
&gt;&gt; Welcome my name is De Antonella,
I'm from Lima Peru,

00:04:33.088 --> 00:04:36.621
I was at the GSB between 2016 and 18.

00:04:36.621 --> 00:04:39.878
During that time I incubated
a social enterprise called

00:04:39.878 --> 00:04:44.726
Latin American Leadership Academy, which
I have been running full time ever since

00:04:44.726 --> 00:04:48.385
through epidemic,
which has been a lot of fun.

00:04:48.385 --> 00:04:52.183
So happy to answer questions around,
the social impact,

00:04:52.183 --> 00:04:56.380
the social innovation,
the entrepreneurship side of the GSB.

00:04:58.490 --> 00:05:00.182
How the professors,

00:05:00.182 --> 00:05:05.634
the alumni community have been
instrumental in my journey and more.

00:05:05.634 --> 00:05:06.512
Great to meet you all.

00:05:08.875 --> 00:05:10.653
&gt;&gt; I think that makes me next.

00:05:10.653 --> 00:05:14.168
I'm Ilana, I was GSB class of 2020, and

00:05:14.168 --> 00:05:20.710
I am now talking at you from Houston Texas
where I'm a consultant for Dane.

00:05:20.710 --> 00:05:24.458
Before that I grew up in Portland, Oregon.

00:05:24.458 --> 00:05:28.488
Went to a tiny college called
the Olin College of Engineering.

00:05:28.488 --> 00:05:32.740
And by tiny, I mean,
80 people a year, truly tiny.

00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:34.673
And everyone was an engineer.

00:05:34.673 --> 00:05:40.010
Ran away from engineering, did a masters
in England, in European literature.

00:05:40.010 --> 00:05:45.454
And then a fulbright year also focused
on literature and theater in Germany,

00:05:45.454 --> 00:05:50.394
I followed that up with working as
a product manager in tech for Yahoo,

00:05:50.394 --> 00:05:52.770
and then came to the GSB.

00:05:52.770 --> 00:05:55.023
And who knows where I'm going next?

00:05:55.023 --> 00:05:59.610
But I had a great time taking advantage of
all the things that GSB had to offer, so

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:02.160
looking forward to
talking to you about it.

00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:08.620
&gt;&gt; Hi everyone my name is Jorge,
I'm GSB class of 2020, and

00:06:08.620 --> 00:06:11.270
I'm originally from Peru also from Lima.

00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:14.664
But I grew up in Orange County
in Southern California where

00:06:14.664 --> 00:06:16.683
I am right now visiting my family.

00:06:16.683 --> 00:06:22.226
And I currently live full time in
San Francisco, so close by to the GSB.

00:06:22.226 --> 00:06:26.621
I did my undergrad at Stanford where I
studied computer science and economics for

00:06:26.621 --> 00:06:27.830
undergrad.

00:06:27.830 --> 00:06:32.192
Then I did a computer science
master's degree also at Stanford.

00:06:32.192 --> 00:06:37.277
And then actually during my CS masters,
I worked on an interdisciplinary

00:06:37.277 --> 00:06:42.370
class in the school of engineering with
a couple of students from the GSB.

00:06:42.370 --> 00:06:46.688
And actually that's one of the big factors
that motivated me to apply to the GSB

00:06:46.688 --> 00:06:48.290
program, so I did.

00:06:48.290 --> 00:06:50.542
Both of my graduate
degrees at Stanford but

00:06:50.542 --> 00:06:54.490
not through the dual program because I
applied while I was doing the CS masters.

00:06:54.490 --> 00:06:58.615
But I just really enjoyed the perspective
that the GSB students brought in terms of

00:06:58.615 --> 00:07:02.320
their business knowledge as well
as their interpersonal skills.

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:06.509
So I wanted to learn the same skills they
seemed to have gained at the GSB, and

00:07:06.509 --> 00:07:10.120
that's what motivated me to apply and
have a background in tech.

00:07:10.120 --> 00:07:14.197
I work as a product manager, and
I'm particularly interested in emerging

00:07:14.197 --> 00:07:18.030
technologies like artificial
intelligence and blockchain crypto.

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:22.140
&gt;&gt; Hi, everyone, I'm Josh,

00:07:22.140 --> 00:07:25.800
I graduated about three weeks
ago from the Stanford GSB.

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:26.895
As an undergrad,

00:07:26.895 --> 00:07:32.297
I was a bioengineering major with plans to
become this physician scientist, MD, PhD,

00:07:32.297 --> 00:07:37.600
professor, I quickly realized that maybe
that wasn't exactly what I wanted to do.

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:42.530
And so I took a leave from the MD PhD
program at Johns Hopkins to launch

00:07:42.530 --> 00:07:43.915
my first startup,

00:07:43.915 --> 00:07:48.689
a kidney disease diagnostics
company as a technical co founder.

00:07:48.689 --> 00:07:51.698
But I quickly realized that I actually
wanted to be on the business side

00:07:51.698 --> 00:07:52.570
of things.

00:07:52.570 --> 00:07:56.159
And so that's why I knew that I had this
chance to Stanford GSB so I could learn

00:07:56.159 --> 00:08:00.800
the business skills and build a network
necessary to make those dreams come true.

00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:04.338
And so now that I've graduated,
I am the co-founder and

00:08:04.338 --> 00:08:06.845
CEO of Blink Biotechnologies, which is

00:08:06.845 --> 00:08:12.540
a biotech startup based here in San
Francisco, where I'm currently located.

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:14.980
&gt;&gt; Hi everyone, great to be here.

00:08:14.980 --> 00:08:16.573
I'm Sebastian.

00:08:16.573 --> 00:08:17.464
I'm from Mexico City.

00:08:17.464 --> 00:08:18.880
That's where I am now.

00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:22.120
I graduated from the GSB in 2017.

00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:25.987
I did a dual degree with
the Kennedy School at Harvard University,

00:08:25.987 --> 00:08:28.473
which is School of Government at Harvard,

00:08:28.473 --> 00:08:32.840
where I studied a master's in economics
and international development.

00:08:33.970 --> 00:08:38.667
Before that grad school stand tight,
I actually worked for my undergraduate

00:08:38.667 --> 00:08:43.220
institution for Yale, hoping to
invest in the university's endowment,

00:08:43.220 --> 00:08:46.940
things kind around natural resources and
energy.

00:08:46.940 --> 00:08:49.952
And now I do that in Mexico and
Latin America,

00:08:49.952 --> 00:08:56.030
doing investments in decarbonization and
renewable energy, basically.

00:08:56.030 --> 00:08:59.390
So happy to talk about what
it's like to do a dual degree,

00:08:59.390 --> 00:09:02.403
especially when you're
not in the same location,

00:09:02.403 --> 00:09:06.905
and moving across places, or anything
else that you guys want to talk about.

00:09:06.905 --> 00:09:09.790
&gt;&gt; Great, well, thank you.

00:09:09.790 --> 00:09:13.710
I'll start us off with a few questions
before we go to our audience questions.

00:09:13.710 --> 00:09:16.528
So again, our audience,
if you have anything on your mind,

00:09:16.528 --> 00:09:20.020
please feel free to go to that Q&amp;A box and
type some questions in.

00:09:20.020 --> 00:09:23.395
But a general question for
all of our panelists here,

00:09:23.395 --> 00:09:28.050
what was one of your favorite classes or
academic experiences, and why?

00:09:29.430 --> 00:09:34.045
&gt;&gt; My favorite class of the GSB was
reputation management by Allison Kluger,

00:09:34.045 --> 00:09:37.634
which is a class where you
actually ask your classmates and

00:09:37.634 --> 00:09:42.103
other people that are important to you
in your life how they view you, and

00:09:42.103 --> 00:09:44.280
what their perception of you is.

00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:46.186
And it was one of the best ways for

00:09:46.186 --> 00:09:49.362
me to get feedback about how
I show up in the world, and

00:09:49.362 --> 00:09:53.770
how I could decide to change or
not change different aspects of myself.

00:09:53.770 --> 00:09:56.189
And so I gained so much from that class.

00:09:56.189 --> 00:10:00.572
And it was a really good experiential
class in which you really went out and

00:10:00.572 --> 00:10:03.270
sought feedback on how
to improve yourself.

00:10:05.030 --> 00:10:06.901
&gt;&gt; This is hard, because I have five, and

00:10:06.901 --> 00:10:10.362
I was really hoping that other people
would go first and say some of them,

00:10:10.362 --> 00:10:13.710
so that I could say whichever
one hadn't been covered.

00:10:13.710 --> 00:10:18.711
But if I have to pick one, I'm going to
pick dynamics of the global wine industry,

00:10:18.711 --> 00:10:20.680
which is taught by Alyssa Rapp.

00:10:20.680 --> 00:10:24.508
It is a great class, so great,
that I essentially took it twice,

00:10:24.508 --> 00:10:27.110
once as a student, and once as a TA.

00:10:27.110 --> 00:10:31.565
And it's not just because you taste
a lot of really great wine in class.

00:10:31.565 --> 00:10:34.858
Although you do, and
that's definitely a benefit.

00:10:34.858 --> 00:10:39.148
But I also think that that
class was actually the best

00:10:39.148 --> 00:10:41.889
strategy class I took at the GSB.

00:10:41.889 --> 00:10:44.514
We have a lot of foundation
strategy classes,

00:10:44.514 --> 00:10:47.891
which should give you a lot of
frameworks which are useful.

00:10:47.891 --> 00:10:52.794
But this really applied it all to
an industry that is really difficult to

00:10:52.794 --> 00:10:56.530
navigate, right, global, highly regulated.

00:10:56.530 --> 00:11:00.546
It deals with kind of all of the problems
you could imagine an industry has to

00:11:00.546 --> 00:11:01.960
deal with.

00:11:01.960 --> 00:11:06.066
And learning from people, and
really brainstorming ways to build

00:11:06.066 --> 00:11:10.774
successful businesses in spite of that
was a really great education for me.

00:11:10.774 --> 00:11:15.128
Also, Alyssa Rapp is an amazing human
being, and well worth taking a class with,

00:11:15.128 --> 00:11:16.400
just to get to meet her.

00:11:17.430 --> 00:11:21.000
&gt;&gt; All right, this next one, Sebastian,
I'll have you start us off here.

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:24.965
We talked about how the Stanford GSB
utilizes a variety of teaching methods,

00:11:24.965 --> 00:11:27.467
ranging from case to role plays,
simulations,

00:11:27.467 --> 00:11:29.900
all sorts of different things here.

00:11:29.900 --> 00:11:34.027
How do you think that enhanced your
learning experience during your time at

00:11:34.027 --> 00:11:35.530
Stanford?

00:11:35.530 --> 00:11:40.260
&gt;&gt; Sure, no, I think as I was reflecting
for the seminar, I was really thinking of

00:11:40.260 --> 00:11:45.080
the exact point that you're making on
the diversity of classes that you take on.

00:11:45.080 --> 00:11:50.732
So when I think of the types of classes,
I took a class that's kind of GSB,

00:11:50.732 --> 00:11:56.659
and part of the design code school
called design for extreme affordability,

00:11:56.659 --> 00:12:03.160
where we were literally using our hands to
build a fertilizer for farmers in Africa.

00:12:03.160 --> 00:12:08.188
We took a very hardcore finance class
that feels very much as you're in

00:12:08.188 --> 00:12:14.328
a lecture listening to a hugely successful
professor on financial economic theory,

00:12:14.328 --> 00:12:20.916
and probably not understanding 50% of what
is happening, but you're in that lecture.

00:12:20.916 --> 00:12:26.186
And then you also have classes that
are really about kind of role playing,

00:12:26.186 --> 00:12:29.331
and putting yourself in a practicing mode,

00:12:29.331 --> 00:12:32.908
things that now in my career,
I really value now.

00:12:32.908 --> 00:12:38.036
Also, I took a class, for example,
called Managing Difficult Conversations

00:12:38.036 --> 00:12:42.244
with with Eric Groesbeck,
who's probably one of the kind of most

00:12:42.244 --> 00:12:47.010
famous professors at the GSB, and
you're practicing firing people.

00:12:47.010 --> 00:12:50.316
And so now [LAUGH] in my job,
I had never fired someone before, and

00:12:50.316 --> 00:12:51.981
I had to recently fire someone.

00:12:51.981 --> 00:12:55.900
And I was immediately
thinking of the practice I at

00:12:55.900 --> 00:12:58.801
least had doing that once at the GSB.

00:12:58.801 --> 00:13:01.657
And then you also have classes
like startup garage, and

00:13:01.657 --> 00:13:05.792
these kinds of classes where you're
literally kind of building your business,

00:13:05.792 --> 00:13:09.280
and have an incredible amount of
resources to build that business.

00:13:09.280 --> 00:13:13.826
So my wife also went to the GSB, and she
now has a startup that she started at in

00:13:13.826 --> 00:13:18.590
this class, startup garage, and was able
to spend six months during her second

00:13:18.590 --> 00:13:21.390
year getting credit for
building that class.

00:13:21.390 --> 00:13:26.357
And so in addition to having that
diversity of literally every kind of type

00:13:26.357 --> 00:13:30.924
of education you can imagine,
I also think that diversity actually

00:13:30.924 --> 00:13:35.189
translates to very real life
valuable experience post-GSB.

00:13:35.189 --> 00:13:38.776
And that kind of diversity has been,
at least for me,

00:13:38.776 --> 00:13:41.980
really critical in my
career post-Stanford.

00:13:41.980 --> 00:13:45.120
So hopefully that answered your questions.

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:52.398
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, this next question all
point towards Alana, Diego, and Josh.

00:13:52.398 --> 00:13:54.625
What was your relationship
like with faculty?

00:13:54.625 --> 00:13:58.416
How did you interact with them in or
out of the class in different ways?

00:13:58.416 --> 00:14:01.370
Alana, maybe if you'd
like to start us off?

00:14:01.370 --> 00:14:05.129
&gt;&gt; Sure, well, you take a lot
of classes at once at the GSB.

00:14:05.129 --> 00:14:09.886
So the reality is you're not going to be
best friends with every single faculty

00:14:09.886 --> 00:14:10.950
member.

00:14:10.950 --> 00:14:14.769
But I did definitely take time outside of
class to get to know particular faculty

00:14:14.769 --> 00:14:19.440
members when I loved their class, or where
I felt a strong connection with them.

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:24.719
And that looked like going to office hours
sometimes, but it also just looked like

00:14:24.719 --> 00:14:30.720
asking them to take some random time with
me, and I've never had anyone say no.

00:14:30.720 --> 00:14:34.090
And usually,
they just desperately want to help you.

00:14:34.090 --> 00:14:38.772
One reason I really love Alyssa Rapp
is I showed up to this one on one

00:14:38.772 --> 00:14:41.338
that I had asked her to have with me.

00:14:41.338 --> 00:14:45.327
She sat down, and she said okay, what do
you want to do during your internship?

00:14:45.327 --> 00:14:47.258
What do you want to do post-GSB?

00:14:47.258 --> 00:14:51.219
And I said well, I want to explore
working for a performing arts nonprofit.

00:14:51.219 --> 00:14:53.697
I'm really hoping to get
an internship there.

00:14:53.697 --> 00:14:58.089
She went great, here are four leaders
of performing arts nonprofits but

00:14:58.089 --> 00:15:00.108
I have personal connections to.

00:15:00.108 --> 00:15:03.060
Which ones would you like
email introductions to?

00:15:03.060 --> 00:15:07.961
And that was actually how I got my summer
internship at a major ballet company,

00:15:07.961 --> 00:15:11.470
absolutely no relation to
what she was teaching.

00:15:11.470 --> 00:15:14.520
I'd expected we were going to
talk wine in her class.

00:15:14.520 --> 00:15:18.718
But what she really just wanted was
to be useful to me as a student.

00:15:18.718 --> 00:15:22.516
And I found that across
the professors that I connected with.

00:15:22.516 --> 00:15:25.502
And I really loved getting
to know them better, and

00:15:25.502 --> 00:15:28.981
having them wanting to marshal
their resources to help me.

00:15:28.981 --> 00:15:33.760
That's a hugely valuable asset
while you're at the GSB.

00:15:33.760 --> 00:15:37.010
&gt;&gt; Diego, how about your experience
interacting with faculty?

00:15:38.688 --> 00:15:40.708
&gt;&gt; How fantastic [LAUGH].

00:15:40.708 --> 00:15:46.120
So I remember in my interview,
I was told that one of the most

00:15:46.120 --> 00:15:51.004
underutilized assets of
the GSB are the professors.

00:15:51.004 --> 00:15:55.926
And I kind of blew it off
as a marketing stunt.

00:15:55.926 --> 00:15:58.702
And At the GSB and
then especially post GSB,

00:15:58.702 --> 00:16:01.990
I'm blown away by the caliber
of the professors.

00:16:01.990 --> 00:16:07.496
They truly are leaders in their
industries and amazing people.

00:16:07.496 --> 00:16:11.566
So some GSB professors
have been unofficial and

00:16:11.566 --> 00:16:17.955
official advisors for Laila,
the social enterprise I founded.

00:16:17.955 --> 00:16:22.936
Some have been donors,
I'm not going to tell you whom,

00:16:22.936 --> 00:16:26.465
some helped us find our main donors.

00:16:26.465 --> 00:16:30.915
Some helped us get foundation support
in the foundation that they worked at.

00:16:33.070 --> 00:16:34.580
Yeah, they've been amazing.

00:16:34.580 --> 00:16:38.300
And also know in hindsight,
Lisa, that, for example,

00:16:38.300 --> 00:16:43.430
we're trying to find certain funders or
to get to work with some foundations.

00:16:43.430 --> 00:16:46.509
I'm like, it's so
hard to get to Eric Schmidt now.

00:16:46.509 --> 00:16:49.365
It would have been nicer
to take his class, or

00:16:49.365 --> 00:16:54.190
I would have been nicer to take the class
with say the CEO of Omidyar Network.

00:16:54.190 --> 00:16:57.040
Now, it's really hard
that I'm on the outside.

00:16:57.040 --> 00:17:01.473
So definitely wonderful professors and
great opportunity to get to

00:17:01.473 --> 00:17:05.531
meet them while you're a student
at the GSB outside of class.

00:17:05.531 --> 00:17:08.995
&gt;&gt; Josh, how about your experience
as of interacting with faculty?

00:17:10.780 --> 00:17:14.948
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I've had amazing experiences with
faculty both sort of in the classroom

00:17:14.948 --> 00:17:19.177
setting related to the academics, as well
as outside related to my startup, and

00:17:19.177 --> 00:17:21.290
entrepreneurial endeavors.

00:17:21.290 --> 00:17:24.067
And I served as a TA for multiple faculty.

00:17:24.067 --> 00:17:29.124
I did a independent study with a faculty
member on kidney disease in the US and

00:17:29.124 --> 00:17:33.706
healthcare system, where I was able
to work with five other my fellow

00:17:33.706 --> 00:17:36.960
classmates on this
independent study project.

00:17:36.960 --> 00:17:41.686
Really dive deep into one area of
healthcare that I was really passionate

00:17:41.686 --> 00:17:42.230
about.

00:17:42.230 --> 00:17:46.912
And having supportive faculty through
as into the academic project was

00:17:46.912 --> 00:17:50.813
really valuable in terms of
getting access to people who had

00:17:50.813 --> 00:17:56.360
information that maybe I would not
have been able to talk to, otherwise.

00:17:56.360 --> 00:17:58.964
And even for my entrepreneurial endeavors,

00:17:58.964 --> 00:18:03.551
I have to say that the faculty of the GSB
both are just so kind and willing to help,

00:18:03.551 --> 00:18:07.770
but also so well-connected and
successful in their own right.

00:18:07.770 --> 00:18:13.410
And so what, some one of the faculty
members is an investor in my company now.

00:18:13.410 --> 00:18:15.479
One of them I use as a reference check for

00:18:15.479 --> 00:18:18.007
my investors who were
considering investing.

00:18:18.007 --> 00:18:20.229
And that investor told
me that they were so

00:18:20.229 --> 00:18:24.481
impressed that this faculty members that
such good things about me that that was

00:18:24.481 --> 00:18:27.730
a one of the reasons that they
decided to invest, actually.

00:18:29.370 --> 00:18:35.088
And another faculty was actually knew one
of my potential board members and was able

00:18:35.088 --> 00:18:41.170
to provide me with deep insight into how
this person operated as a board member.

00:18:41.170 --> 00:18:43.404
And so truly, I think the resources and

00:18:43.404 --> 00:18:46.528
the value you can get from
the faculty relationships.

00:18:46.528 --> 00:18:49.970
I think this runs the entire
gamut of all possible things.

00:18:49.970 --> 00:18:54.761
And so, I've had a really great experience
getting to know the faculty I've

00:18:54.761 --> 00:18:59.715
taken the classes with and even those
ones I haven't even taking classes with.

00:18:59.715 --> 00:19:01.424
&gt;&gt; Great, the specs questions.

00:19:01.424 --> 00:19:05.994
&gt;&gt; If there's just one thing I would add
just for more of a social dynamic with

00:19:05.994 --> 00:19:11.720
professors, these my housemates and I,
our second year people, live in houses.

00:19:11.720 --> 00:19:15.398
And so, we did this, but
a lot of people do this every Wednesday.

00:19:15.398 --> 00:19:19.129
We would invite a different professor
to come have dinner with us and

00:19:19.129 --> 00:19:23.188
they'd spend three to five hours with
us having dinner, drinking wine,

00:19:23.188 --> 00:19:27.050
talking about academic things,
talking about their lives.

00:19:27.050 --> 00:19:30.746
And I was just always so blown away by the
willingness of people to just kind of do

00:19:30.746 --> 00:19:33.350
that and engage with you and
in a more social setting.

00:19:33.350 --> 00:19:35.909
Now, in addition to the points,
so I, Johnston, and

00:19:35.909 --> 00:19:37.704
David brought up a bit more academic.

00:19:37.704 --> 00:19:38.740
This was just a purely

00:19:38.740 --> 00:19:42.125
social experience that was wonderful
&gt;&gt; Wonderful.

00:19:42.125 --> 00:19:43.846
I've heard many of those stories too.

00:19:43.846 --> 00:19:47.685
Some of the top US economic advisors who
are faculty are off mountain biking during

00:19:47.685 --> 00:19:49.521
the weekend with students and things,

00:19:49.521 --> 00:19:54.020
I think they're just wonderful connections
that are made during your time there.

00:19:54.020 --> 00:19:57.560
And Jorge, this next question,
we'll have you start us off here.

00:19:57.560 --> 00:20:02.350
What are some ways that students can help
shape the academic experience, either for

00:20:02.350 --> 00:20:05.060
themselves or through others?

00:20:05.060 --> 00:20:07.688
&gt;&gt; Yeah, there's so many different
ways that you can get involved with

00:20:07.688 --> 00:20:09.060
shaping the academic experience.

00:20:09.060 --> 00:20:13.622
One of the big ways that I participated
in during my time at Stanford GSB was as

00:20:13.622 --> 00:20:18.472
a member of the academic committee, which
is a part of the GSB student government

00:20:18.472 --> 00:20:21.635
that focuses on shaping
the academics experience.

00:20:21.635 --> 00:20:25.275
Serving as a liaison between
the students and the faculty, and

00:20:25.275 --> 00:20:30.017
that included things like collecting
feedback from students about the different

00:20:30.017 --> 00:20:32.986
classes, especially with
a focus on first years.

00:20:32.986 --> 00:20:37.823
Making sure that people feel like they're
being supported in the first quarter as

00:20:37.823 --> 00:20:42.798
they're transitioning into the GSB to make
sure they have the tutoring support that

00:20:42.798 --> 00:20:45.764
they need for
some of the more difficult classes.

00:20:45.764 --> 00:20:50.512
To make sure that professors are providing
a type of curriculum that engages

00:20:50.512 --> 00:20:55.483
students and resonates with students both
in terms of the material with regards

00:20:55.483 --> 00:20:57.719
to the companies being discussed.

00:20:57.719 --> 00:21:02.011
As well as the diversity of the content
focusing on female founders,

00:21:02.011 --> 00:21:05.859
minority entrepreneurs,
that type of thing to make sure that

00:21:05.859 --> 00:21:10.302
the content appeals to a broad range
of students from all backgrounds.

00:21:10.302 --> 00:21:14.766
In addition to that, I worked on
organizing our quarterly academic small

00:21:14.766 --> 00:21:20.030
group dinners, which connected small
groups of students with a faculty member.

00:21:20.030 --> 00:21:24.010
And we provided funding for that for
students to get reimbursed for

00:21:24.010 --> 00:21:28.345
either going out to dinner or ordering
dinner to be at the dorms that are at

00:21:28.345 --> 00:21:30.780
their house with different faculties.

00:21:30.780 --> 00:21:34.629
So this is a great opportunity for
students to connect with particular

00:21:34.629 --> 00:21:38.870
faculty members, whether it's from
their existing classes, or people who

00:21:38.870 --> 00:21:43.327
they actually haven't taken the class
as up-to-date want to learn more about.

00:21:43.327 --> 00:21:48.095
And this is a great opportunity to build
those more personal relations with faculty

00:21:48.095 --> 00:21:52.522
because sometimes they'll be willing
to share some more personal aspects of

00:21:52.522 --> 00:21:53.271
their life or

00:21:53.271 --> 00:21:57.980
their background that you might not get
to hear about within the classroom.

00:21:57.980 --> 00:21:59.174
And for me, actually,

00:21:59.174 --> 00:22:02.899
some of those dinners helped me learn
about professors in a way that like.

00:22:02.899 --> 00:22:06.628
I guess when I took their class maybe
I didn't expect I would connect with

00:22:06.628 --> 00:22:10.124
them on such a personal level, but
speaking to them during dinner.

00:22:10.124 --> 00:22:14.226
I found that we had a lot in common and
then their professors that I've maintained

00:22:14.226 --> 00:22:16.893
contact with, and
I'm close with in the long term.

00:22:16.893 --> 00:22:19.955
And then, another great way to
get involved is connecting your

00:22:19.955 --> 00:22:24.002
experience with the different student
groups, to your academic experience, and

00:22:24.002 --> 00:22:25.460
that's something I did.

00:22:25.460 --> 00:22:28.835
I was one of the co-founders of
the Stanford Blockchain Club,

00:22:28.835 --> 00:22:31.956
which is an undergraduate and
graduate interest group for

00:22:31.956 --> 00:22:35.476
people who are interested in
blockchain and cryptocurrencies.

00:22:35.476 --> 00:22:38.957
And there's a couple of professors
who were starting crypto and

00:22:38.957 --> 00:22:40.569
blockchain classes my year,

00:22:40.569 --> 00:22:44.901
and they didn't have that much technical
or academic experience in the space.

00:22:44.901 --> 00:22:49.190
But from my computer science engineering
degree, I had worked with Dan Boney,

00:22:49.190 --> 00:22:52.020
who's one of the top
researchers in the field.

00:22:52.020 --> 00:22:56.863
So I helped make a lot of the connections,
both to academics in the field as well as

00:22:56.863 --> 00:23:00.940
to entrepreneurs working in
the blockchain space to get speakers.

00:23:00.940 --> 00:23:05.565
And to figure out case studies for the
various classes, as well as to host small

00:23:05.565 --> 00:23:09.981
group events to connect students to
the speakers that came to visit in a more

00:23:09.981 --> 00:23:14.634
intimate setting after we have these
classes with the guests that we invited.

00:23:14.634 --> 00:23:19.261
And then, through my experience as
a first-generation and low-income student,

00:23:19.261 --> 00:23:22.468
I also founded the First Gen
Low Income Stanford GSB Club.

00:23:22.468 --> 00:23:26.675
And through this club, I help to advocate
for providing more resources and

00:23:26.675 --> 00:23:30.102
opportunities for
students who come from this background.

00:23:30.102 --> 00:23:34.587
Who were the first to go to college from
their families who may need additional

00:23:34.587 --> 00:23:39.348
financial support because they have a lot
of financial stresses from the families

00:23:39.348 --> 00:23:40.930
back home.

00:23:40.930 --> 00:23:43.810
And in this way, for
example, we advocated for

00:23:43.810 --> 00:23:48.850
additional tutoring sessions maybe for
students who came from different academic

00:23:48.850 --> 00:23:53.389
backgrounds to make sure that they were
supported as they started the GSB.

00:23:53.389 --> 00:23:56.850
As well as additional ways to
provide financial support,

00:23:56.850 --> 00:23:59.816
especially to students who
maybe have families or

00:23:59.816 --> 00:24:03.010
other dependents that
they're supporting as well.

00:24:03.010 --> 00:24:04.270
So basically like.

00:24:04.270 --> 00:24:08.405
The GSB provides so many opportunities for
you to take the initiative and

00:24:08.405 --> 00:24:09.845
shape your experience.

00:24:09.845 --> 00:24:12.624
And the experience of your peers
in whatever way you want, and

00:24:12.624 --> 00:24:15.091
I actually just thought of
two other really cool ways.

00:24:15.091 --> 00:24:17.227
But in my GSB experience,

00:24:17.227 --> 00:24:22.971
I also helped to lead the Stanford AI
salon which was a speaker series.

00:24:22.971 --> 00:24:27.906
That brings in speakers from
the artificial intelligence field.

00:24:27.906 --> 00:24:31.098
And that was a great way to connect
artificial intelligence from

00:24:31.098 --> 00:24:32.408
a technical standpoint.

00:24:32.408 --> 00:24:36.839
To the social impact that that
has across the world, and

00:24:36.839 --> 00:24:40.050
in terms of economics, social issues.

00:24:40.050 --> 00:24:45.647
And then also through that I helped
to advise the FDA on their policies

00:24:45.647 --> 00:24:51.164
around regulating artificial
intelligence in a medical context.

00:24:51.164 --> 00:24:54.365
So that was also experience
related to academics.

00:24:54.365 --> 00:24:57.828
&gt;&gt; Nice, nice, I want to build
off something that Jorge said.

00:24:57.828 --> 00:25:01.704
Kind of in how you were talking
about the block chain course,

00:25:01.704 --> 00:25:03.612
the AI kind of interactions.

00:25:03.612 --> 00:25:05.279
We talked about how Stanford,

00:25:05.279 --> 00:25:09.710
GSB is part of that much larger
ecosystem at Stanford University.

00:25:09.710 --> 00:25:14.211
So maybe Alana and Sebastian,
if you want to tackle this question.

00:25:14.211 --> 00:25:16.555
Did you take advantage of classes or

00:25:16.555 --> 00:25:21.810
learning opportunities outside
the business school or elsewhere?

00:25:21.810 --> 00:25:26.179
And tell us a little bit more about that,
Alana, do you want to start?

00:25:26.179 --> 00:25:30.264
&gt;&gt; Well, yes, I took quite if
you across the street classes,

00:25:30.264 --> 00:25:34.773
which is what we call GSB years
taking classes outside of the GSB.

00:25:34.773 --> 00:25:39.077
One was business related and
really to supplement my GSB education.

00:25:39.077 --> 00:25:42.800
And that was the negotiation
class at Stanford Law School.

00:25:42.800 --> 00:25:46.226
The GSB also has a negotiation class
the law school, one is hot and

00:25:46.226 --> 00:25:48.538
a little bit of different format and
style.

00:25:48.538 --> 00:25:52.060
So I thought it would suit me better and
it was a wonderful class,

00:25:52.060 --> 00:25:54.100
I was very happy with it.

00:25:54.100 --> 00:25:57.540
And then I took a bunch of across
the street classes just for fun,

00:25:57.540 --> 00:26:00.820
to get a change from
the business school curriculum.

00:26:00.820 --> 00:26:06.433
So I took some pretty obscure like Spanish
and Portuguese pastoral literature.

00:26:06.433 --> 00:26:11.380
And many people retellings
about particular myth.

00:26:11.380 --> 00:26:14.906
And it was really just to meet
other Stanford people, and

00:26:14.906 --> 00:26:17.265
get my head out of the business space.

00:26:17.265 --> 00:26:21.190
And into something that's already been,
always been really fun for me.

00:26:22.880 --> 00:26:24.455
&gt;&gt; Great, and Sebastian,

00:26:24.455 --> 00:26:28.962
you want to talk about your experiences
outside of the business school?

00:26:28.962 --> 00:26:32.911
&gt;&gt; Yeah, sure, so right,
I took several classes outside of school,

00:26:32.911 --> 00:26:37.503
so outside of the business school within
Stanford that I won't elaborate on.

00:26:37.503 --> 00:26:44.029
But I think one of the big experiences for
me was combining the MBA curriculum and

00:26:44.029 --> 00:26:48.816
this other degree at Harvard,
focused on economics etc.

00:26:48.816 --> 00:26:52.680
And so
I think that was challenging for sure,

00:26:52.680 --> 00:26:57.674
because you're moving across
the country [LAUGH] three times.

00:26:57.674 --> 00:27:00.534
You need to miss out on certain things,

00:27:00.534 --> 00:27:07.170
because there's a quarter when you need to
be at the Kennedy School and vice versa.

00:27:07.170 --> 00:27:09.493
But beyond those kind of
logistical issues,

00:27:09.493 --> 00:27:12.576
I think it really enhanced
the academic experience I had.

00:27:12.576 --> 00:27:16.031
I was able to take a lot of insights and
things that I was maybe thinking from

00:27:16.031 --> 00:27:18.501
a top public policy
perspective at Kennedy school.

00:27:18.501 --> 00:27:19.501
And then bring them back and

00:27:19.501 --> 00:27:22.205
think about them more from
the business school perspective.

00:27:22.205 --> 00:27:24.532
I had professors who knew
each other at both schools.

00:27:24.532 --> 00:27:28.934
And that really created kind of a unique
way to interact with each of them,

00:27:28.934 --> 00:27:31.630
and actually jointly on a few things.

00:27:31.630 --> 00:27:36.120
So I think that for me,
that just really enhanced the experience.

00:27:36.120 --> 00:27:38.778
And in addition,
it was just incredible to see the support

00:27:38.778 --> 00:27:41.690
that you get from the GSB to be
able to do those kinds of things.

00:27:41.690 --> 00:27:46.551
And I think evidence of that is that now
one in five I think is what Lisa said,

00:27:46.551 --> 00:27:49.187
of students do some sort of dual degree.

00:27:49.187 --> 00:27:53.817
Or for joint degree comm, so
it's become kind of a part of

00:27:53.817 --> 00:27:58.264
the community that is very normal,
very supportive.

00:27:58.264 --> 00:28:02.164
And for me, personally very
enhancing to my education, so

00:28:02.164 --> 00:28:03.958
I really grateful I did it.

00:28:03.958 --> 00:28:04.536
&gt;&gt; Great.

00:28:04.536 --> 00:28:05.377
&gt;&gt; Building on that,

00:28:05.377 --> 00:28:08.592
I think one of the things that I liked
the most about the GSB experiences.

00:28:08.592 --> 00:28:12.698
Both the flexibility of the academic
experience to tailor it

00:28:12.698 --> 00:28:16.572
to exactly what you need and
want out of your experience.

00:28:16.572 --> 00:28:20.116
As well as the close integration between
the GSB and these other schools.

00:28:20.116 --> 00:28:21.704
With regards to the flexibility,

00:28:21.704 --> 00:28:24.781
one of the cool things is that
basically after your first quarter.

00:28:24.781 --> 00:28:29.675
You always have some room in your schedule
to be able to fit in any classes you want

00:28:29.675 --> 00:28:31.734
from any other part of Stanford.

00:28:31.734 --> 00:28:34.856
And the nice thing about it is that
you're not restricted by like,

00:28:34.856 --> 00:28:37.310
you can only take a certain
number of classes.

00:28:37.310 --> 00:28:41.966
You can basically fit in as many across
the street classes as you can fit within

00:28:41.966 --> 00:28:45.710
your maximum units that
you're allotted for Stanford.

00:28:45.710 --> 00:28:48.729
So you can essentially take
as many classes as you want,

00:28:48.729 --> 00:28:51.630
as long as your meeting all
of your GSP requirements.

00:28:51.630 --> 00:28:53.873
And as long as you're within
the maximum allowed, and

00:28:53.873 --> 00:28:55.750
I definitely took advantage of that.

00:28:55.750 --> 00:28:59.095
I took classes, even more classes
in the computer science department.

00:28:59.095 --> 00:29:02.070
Because there's just so
many cool classes which engineering.

00:29:02.070 --> 00:29:03.656
And some classes in law school,

00:29:03.656 --> 00:29:07.310
which was really cool because I had never
done anything related to law before.

00:29:07.310 --> 00:29:11.672
And that gave me a strong background for
the work I was doing, advising the FDA.

00:29:11.672 --> 00:29:16.630
And then also, I got to explore some
new interests, I took a sailing class.

00:29:16.630 --> 00:29:21.138
There's a bay area near
Stanford in Redwood City, and

00:29:21.138 --> 00:29:23.790
they have a sailing club there.

00:29:23.790 --> 00:29:27.510
So I got to go there a couple of times
each week to go and learn how to sail,

00:29:27.510 --> 00:29:29.120
it's just really cool.

00:29:29.120 --> 00:29:32.366
And then I also took an electronic
music production class.

00:29:32.366 --> 00:29:36.926
Which was a really cool way to connect my
interest in the arts with my experience in

00:29:36.926 --> 00:29:37.740
technology.

00:29:37.740 --> 00:29:42.679
And that was through karma which
was the Stanford Center for

00:29:42.679 --> 00:29:44.668
music and for the arts.

00:29:44.668 --> 00:29:47.394
&gt;&gt; I see, go ahead Alana.

00:29:47.394 --> 00:29:50.219
&gt;&gt; I just want to say one quick thing
which is that I know this an academics

00:29:50.219 --> 00:29:51.780
panel, but it's not just classes.

00:29:51.780 --> 00:29:56.461
One of the things that was really fun, and
I think is underutilized by GSB students.

00:29:56.461 --> 00:29:59.872
Is the ability to get involved
with student organizations that

00:29:59.872 --> 00:30:00.975
are Stanford wide.

00:30:02.475 --> 00:30:03.128
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:30:03.128 --> 00:30:05.419
I think Nate is had a chance to join us,

00:30:05.419 --> 00:30:08.465
would you like to briefly
introduce yourself?

00:30:08.465 --> 00:30:09.660
Welcome.

00:30:09.660 --> 00:30:11.504
&gt;&gt; Yeah, hi everyone, apologies for

00:30:11.504 --> 00:30:14.460
joining tremendously late
coming from something else.

00:30:15.490 --> 00:30:22.770
A class of 16, I came to GSB having
worked for 6 years in corporate finance.

00:30:22.770 --> 00:30:27.181
And then after GSB,
I spent 4 years in consulting and

00:30:27.181 --> 00:30:33.366
now I'm in the venture world in a hybrid
role, but mostly a platform role.

00:30:33.366 --> 00:30:38.553
So working with our LPs and our portfolio
companies within the FinTech and

00:30:38.553 --> 00:30:40.482
banking space to connect.

00:30:40.482 --> 00:30:44.551
And yeah, I just really love Stanford and

00:30:44.551 --> 00:30:50.490
opened a lot of doors for me,
and I was excited to join today.

00:30:50.490 --> 00:30:53.479
So I'm really sad I missed the first
portion but happy to meet you.

00:30:53.479 --> 00:30:54.832
&gt;&gt; Nice we'll catch you out up,

00:30:54.832 --> 00:30:57.552
maybe you could share with us
one of your favorite classes.

00:30:57.552 --> 00:31:00.278
Or academic experiences
during your time at Stanford.

00:31:00.278 --> 00:31:05.600
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I'll share my favorite GSB class
and then my favorite Stanford class.

00:31:05.600 --> 00:31:10.940
So my favorite GSB class was
high performance leadership.

00:31:10.940 --> 00:31:14.025
I think it's what it's called,

00:31:14.025 --> 00:31:19.985
I like to describe it as touchy
feely to or applied touchy feely.

00:31:19.985 --> 00:31:23.592
I really took a lot from that class that

00:31:23.592 --> 00:31:27.774
when I was managing
people as a consultant.

00:31:27.774 --> 00:31:32.713
Came to play both with our clients and
coaching them, but also my own team.

00:31:32.713 --> 00:31:38.655
And then outside the GSB I took,
I forgot the exact name of it,

00:31:38.655 --> 00:31:42.940
but it was an economics theory class.

00:31:42.940 --> 00:31:49.660
And it was, I didn't intend to sign up for
a PhD class, but I'm really glad I did.

00:31:49.660 --> 00:31:52.891
Because we just talked
about things that were so

00:31:52.891 --> 00:31:57.099
out of the norm of what I would
have otherwise had exposure to.

00:31:57.099 --> 00:31:59.207
That I think, opened the door for

00:31:59.207 --> 00:32:04.259
me to understand certain things in
business differently and- I think it

00:32:04.259 --> 00:32:09.937
was like economic theory of
institutions or something like that.

00:32:09.937 --> 00:32:12.792
And scrape rate class so backhoe,

00:32:12.792 --> 00:32:18.014
the take classes outside your
comfort zone across the street.

00:32:18.014 --> 00:32:20.970
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that actually reminds me
of that this other class I took,

00:32:20.970 --> 00:32:24.642
I took a quantum computing class without
any background in quantum computing or

00:32:24.642 --> 00:32:26.692
quantum mechanics or physics or anything.

00:32:26.692 --> 00:32:30.900
And I definitely didn't expect it to be
what was essentially like my favorite

00:32:30.900 --> 00:32:35.108
class ever, but just the way that
the class was integrated with technology,

00:32:35.108 --> 00:32:37.331
physics, math, and even philosophy.

00:32:37.331 --> 00:32:40.939
Like learning about quantum computing
helped me think about the world and

00:32:40.939 --> 00:32:42.334
how people are connected and

00:32:42.334 --> 00:32:46.547
all of the possibilities that could exist
with different universes and everything.

00:32:46.547 --> 00:32:51.209
And it was a very deep experience way
beyond anything that I had expected

00:32:51.209 --> 00:32:53.084
before I decided to take it.

00:32:53.084 --> 00:32:57.772
&gt;&gt; Great, I want to build off one of the
classes I just heard mentioned a minute

00:32:57.772 --> 00:33:01.420
ago and we affectionately refer
to it as touchy feely, but

00:33:01.420 --> 00:33:04.117
its real name is interpersonal dynamics.

00:33:04.117 --> 00:33:08.744
And I noticed in the q&amp;a section
folks were asking about classes like

00:33:08.744 --> 00:33:13.053
interpersonal dynamics or
other great soft skills courses, so

00:33:13.053 --> 00:33:17.857
does anyone want to quickly describe
what interpersonal dynamics is?

00:33:17.857 --> 00:33:21.675
And then also maybe mentioned some of your
other favorite soft skills courses like

00:33:21.675 --> 00:33:24.017
high performance leadership
with triad as well.

00:33:27.957 --> 00:33:31.143
&gt;&gt; I can take that since
I brought it [LAUGH].

00:33:31.143 --> 00:33:37.721
I presume it's still the same since
a few years ago but essentially.

00:33:37.721 --> 00:33:43.317
I would say there's really two overarching
things you get out of the class,

00:33:43.317 --> 00:33:47.093
with a lot of nuance sub
points you can get out of it.

00:33:47.093 --> 00:33:52.841
So the first major thing is,
how do you people experience you?

00:33:52.841 --> 00:33:55.040
Like how do you show up to people?

00:33:55.040 --> 00:33:59.588
And then the second is like,
what assumptions do you make

00:33:59.588 --> 00:34:03.949
about people as you're
interacting with them that May

00:34:03.949 --> 00:34:08.522
influence your behavior for
good, bad, indifferent.

00:34:08.522 --> 00:34:13.774
And the way the class is structured
is you're in a group of about 12.

00:34:13.774 --> 00:34:20.923
And there's a plenary component each week
and then you spend 4 or 5 hours in a room.

00:34:20.923 --> 00:34:25.418
If you're 12 people with
a very loose agenda, and

00:34:25.418 --> 00:34:29.001
it's awkward, and then stuff happens.

00:34:29.001 --> 00:34:33.848
And then you all learn and
to be really honest with each other, and

00:34:33.848 --> 00:34:39.228
there's two facilitators and so for
someone who's hyper structured,

00:34:39.228 --> 00:34:42.249
you're just like you want me to do what?

00:34:42.249 --> 00:34:46.441
And then eventually you kind
of just see that the group

00:34:46.441 --> 00:34:50.645
naturally starts to create learning for
each other.

00:34:50.645 --> 00:34:54.490
And then at the end of the class,
there's a weekend or

00:34:54.490 --> 00:34:59.104
a couple days where you do really
just that, but like for eight or

00:34:59.104 --> 00:35:03.290
12 hours at a time, and
then you do a lot of reflection and

00:35:03.290 --> 00:35:07.333
a lot of writing and
I think it's a really great course.

00:35:07.333 --> 00:35:10.063
If that's a focus of yours.

00:35:10.063 --> 00:35:14.782
And then one other class that
I would add in the soft skills

00:35:14.782 --> 00:35:18.035
area is it was a communications class.

00:35:18.035 --> 00:35:23.065
I don't remember the name of it, but
really, you're just learning how

00:35:23.065 --> 00:35:28.176
to communicate and you have to do a lot
of presentation, a lot of different

00:35:28.176 --> 00:35:32.885
exercises and I think It sounds
like it should be straightforward.

00:35:32.885 --> 00:35:37.668
But it, you actually take a lot out of
it if communication is something you

00:35:37.668 --> 00:35:38.762
want to work on.

00:35:38.762 --> 00:35:43.013
And I believe the instructor
I can't remember her name but

00:35:43.013 --> 00:35:47.700
is someone who's very well known
in the communication coaching

00:35:47.700 --> 00:35:52.315
world has been working with
Stanford students for a long time.

00:35:52.315 --> 00:35:57.903
And I just found her What she
picked up about the nuances,

00:35:57.903 --> 00:36:02.912
not just your verbal,
your oral communication but

00:36:02.912 --> 00:36:08.398
your non-verbal communication
to be really helpful.

00:36:08.398 --> 00:36:11.794
&gt;&gt; We have a great question from
an audience member here of,

00:36:11.794 --> 00:36:16.126
how would you describe the learning
culture and environment at the GSB?

00:36:16.126 --> 00:36:21.473
Let's describe the learning culture and
environment.

00:36:21.473 --> 00:36:25.505
&gt;&gt; I can go I think one of the things that
really appeals to me about the GSB is just

00:36:25.505 --> 00:36:26.956
a collaborative culture.

00:36:26.956 --> 00:36:27.812
I think like.

00:36:27.812 --> 00:36:29.809
It's not cutthroat at all.

00:36:29.809 --> 00:36:35.412
It's not like me against you buying for
professors time or for resources.

00:36:35.412 --> 00:36:40.320
It's like me and you working together
to uplift both of us uplift everyone,

00:36:40.320 --> 00:36:44.016
like what can I do to help you
to improve your experience?

00:36:44.016 --> 00:36:49.119
I think it's all about growing, and
learning together as an entire class, and

00:36:49.119 --> 00:36:54.165
as an entire GSB community, and that's
one of the things that I love the most.

00:36:54.165 --> 00:37:00.244
I've always felt really supported
in terms of like emotional support,

00:37:00.244 --> 00:37:05.639
community support, it just feels
like a family in a lot of ways.

00:37:05.639 --> 00:37:09.153
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I would just add to
that point I've already made and

00:37:09.153 --> 00:37:11.745
people who have graduated more recently.

00:37:11.745 --> 00:37:16.823
I don't know if there's a change but that
there is a great non disclosure policy,

00:37:16.823 --> 00:37:21.328
which which invites what quarter has
said in a very obvious way, right?

00:37:21.328 --> 00:37:24.489
No one is kind of competing when
going to a potential employer,

00:37:24.489 --> 00:37:27.185
whatever to say,
I got this great or that great.

00:37:27.185 --> 00:37:31.935
And so that creates a very kind of
collaborative culture as what we're

00:37:31.935 --> 00:37:32.584
saying.

00:37:32.584 --> 00:37:36.510
The only other thing I would say
about I think a culture that I,

00:37:36.510 --> 00:37:40.831
as I reflect on both my experiences,
both at Harvard and Stanford.

00:37:40.831 --> 00:37:46.065
I think the goal that Stanford has of
having all these kind of soft skill

00:37:46.065 --> 00:37:51.035
classes like interpersonal dynamics
that Nadia was describing,

00:37:51.035 --> 00:37:56.907
create a dynamic where people are
constantly asking each other for feedback.

00:37:56.907 --> 00:38:01.527
Constantly role playing things,
and really trying to just improve

00:38:01.527 --> 00:38:06.714
themselves as human beings that I have
never seen in another environment and

00:38:06.714 --> 00:38:12.389
so that actually becomes a really cool
culture where it's encouraged to reflect,

00:38:12.389 --> 00:38:16.468
to give each other feedback,
to be honest with each other.

00:38:16.468 --> 00:38:19.673
That I think was really unique
at least in what I've seen.

00:38:19.673 --> 00:38:23.504
So, I really enjoyed that as well.

00:38:23.504 --> 00:38:27.409
&gt;&gt; We have another question here of, could
you elaborate on the extent of teamwork

00:38:27.409 --> 00:38:30.397
and leadership opportunities
within the classes you took?

00:38:35.146 --> 00:38:36.774
&gt;&gt; I can go for that.

00:38:36.774 --> 00:38:37.860
So there's actually so

00:38:37.860 --> 00:38:41.017
many classes as people have already
mentioned that startup garage.

00:38:41.017 --> 00:38:44.773
There's also across the street classes
that bring in students from all

00:38:44.773 --> 00:38:46.598
the different Stanford schools.

00:38:46.598 --> 00:38:47.456
To work together.

00:38:47.456 --> 00:38:50.057
So you could work hands-on on
various different projects.

00:38:50.057 --> 00:38:54.927
Like I took an entrepreneurship class in
which we were designing a business and

00:38:54.927 --> 00:38:59.575
I was working with people across GSB,
law school, engineering school and

00:38:59.575 --> 00:39:02.704
we essentially like came
up with a business plan.

00:39:02.704 --> 00:39:07.446
Came up with the financial Statements
created a presentation for

00:39:07.446 --> 00:39:12.446
pitch that we did to actual venture
capitalists from Sandhill road,

00:39:12.446 --> 00:39:14.962
who gave us feedback on our pitch.

00:39:14.962 --> 00:39:16.926
And in each of these experiences,

00:39:16.926 --> 00:39:21.277
the cool thing is that there's many
opportunities for you to take the lead.

00:39:21.277 --> 00:39:24.795
And figure out the dynamic with
the team that you're working on.

00:39:24.795 --> 00:39:29.652
So maybe in some cases, you'll
actually want to be the one that takes

00:39:29.652 --> 00:39:34.694
the lead on something and
you'll gain like leading experience there.

00:39:34.694 --> 00:39:38.809
But also in some cases, actually what's
better for the team as a whole is for

00:39:38.809 --> 00:39:41.121
you to take a step back and
to be a follower or

00:39:41.121 --> 00:39:45.647
to be a supporting team member rather than
the leader in that particular instance.

00:39:45.647 --> 00:39:49.742
So I think that's one of the really cool
learning experiences is that you learn

00:39:49.742 --> 00:39:53.030
that leadership It doesn't just
mean being the one at the top,

00:39:53.030 --> 00:39:56.214
who is managing everything,
and telling people what to do.

00:39:56.214 --> 00:39:58.513
Leadership means doing
whatever is necessary,

00:39:58.513 --> 00:40:00.900
to make sure that your team is successful.

00:40:00.900 --> 00:40:05.341
And that can mean working in a large
variety of roles, which sometimes aren't

00:40:05.341 --> 00:40:08.921
the one that's out in the front
having that very visible role.

00:40:08.921 --> 00:40:13.814
And it can mean being a supporter or
someone who is providing a foundation for

00:40:13.814 --> 00:40:17.080
the other team members
to do their best work.

00:40:17.080 --> 00:40:21.177
And you're able to learn about those
dynamics and see how you can fit in most

00:40:21.177 --> 00:40:25.288
effectively in different situations
through the project based classes.

00:40:27.664 --> 00:40:31.364
&gt;&gt; Here's a question that was
directed towards Sebastian but

00:40:31.364 --> 00:40:34.333
I think anyone is also
welcome to chime in here.

00:40:34.333 --> 00:40:39.557
Which classes and or experiential learning
experiences were the most useful for

00:40:39.557 --> 00:40:42.492
you to really become
a leader in your career.

00:40:48.033 --> 00:40:50.900
&gt;&gt; I think a couple come to mind.

00:40:50.900 --> 00:40:56.216
One is touchy freely, or
sorry, interpersonal dynamics.

00:40:56.216 --> 00:40:59.301
Because I think that gives
you a lot of feedback,

00:40:59.301 --> 00:41:04.193
as Mindy was saying, on how you come
across and what is the impact that certain

00:41:04.193 --> 00:41:08.050
things that you hadn't even thought of or
having on others.

00:41:08.050 --> 00:41:10.784
And so it's a very unique
setting in that front and

00:41:10.784 --> 00:41:14.039
that's helped me at least measure
more carefully how I have

00:41:14.039 --> 00:41:17.660
certain interactions from
a professional standpoint.

00:41:17.660 --> 00:41:20.255
The other one is the one
I alluded to earlier

00:41:20.255 --> 00:41:22.790
the managing difficult conversations.

00:41:22.790 --> 00:41:27.942
Which was one where you're basically
role-playing difficult conversations,

00:41:27.942 --> 00:41:29.062
firing someone,

00:41:29.062 --> 00:41:33.848
having a big discussion with the partner
about how you should split equity.

00:41:33.848 --> 00:41:37.971
Those kinds of things that are at
a theoretical level, very easy to do, but

00:41:37.971 --> 00:41:41.114
once you're actually doing
a role-playing exercise,

00:41:41.114 --> 00:41:45.820
it at least gives you one to have done
that before you're actually doing it live.

00:41:45.820 --> 00:41:50.604
And so I think those two for
me at these were

00:41:50.604 --> 00:41:54.989
have been very valuable in my career.

00:41:54.989 --> 00:41:58.654
&gt;&gt; Something I've had to add that struck
me from my time at the GSB is that.

00:41:58.654 --> 00:42:02.749
Sometimes you hear this idea of, did you
really have to go to the GSB to learn

00:42:02.749 --> 00:42:06.500
about marketing because we
could have bought that book.

00:42:06.500 --> 00:42:09.940
Same for finance or accounting,
same for a lot of things.

00:42:09.940 --> 00:42:14.841
One thing that I really valued was
that you have this combination

00:42:14.841 --> 00:42:16.576
of industry leaders.

00:42:16.576 --> 00:42:23.798
And world class researchers who have
curated all of these resources and

00:42:23.798 --> 00:42:30.177
what I found very impactful was
how consistent the narrative,

00:42:30.177 --> 00:42:36.330
the values, the worldviews the mind sets,
war and leaves.

00:42:36.330 --> 00:42:39.225
I don't know if this is intentional or
no but

00:42:39.225 --> 00:42:43.150
pretty much everywhere at
the GSB I heard this idea of like.

00:42:43.150 --> 00:42:46.040
Hey you know to be a better leader you
should listen to be a better leader you

00:42:46.040 --> 00:42:48.190
should really care for the other person.

00:42:48.190 --> 00:42:53.144
You should be kind you should live
by your values you should this

00:42:53.144 --> 00:42:55.018
you should that sorry.

00:42:55.018 --> 00:42:59.639
I think there's only one class that offers
kind of like the dark side narrative which

00:42:59.639 --> 00:43:01.300
is has to power.

00:43:01.300 --> 00:43:02.738
I didn't take it, but

00:43:02.738 --> 00:43:07.282
I might have something is that this
professor's narrative is, yeah,

00:43:07.282 --> 00:43:12.300
all of that is beautiful but you should
not ignore how power works in the world.

00:43:12.300 --> 00:43:17.189
So this class is focused on power but
everything else was is very consistent

00:43:17.189 --> 00:43:21.680
story like even like how to fire people,
negotiations and so on.

00:43:21.680 --> 00:43:26.930
Everything was very consistent
around like age, be a good person.

00:43:26.930 --> 00:43:31.562
Just bring your best and actually
there's hard evidence that supports it.

00:43:31.562 --> 00:43:35.501
This will help you be a better leader,
a better negotiator, better salesperson,

00:43:35.501 --> 00:43:36.720
better boss and so on.

00:43:36.720 --> 00:43:41.017
And that helped me just cut through
all the noise of so many gurus and

00:43:41.017 --> 00:43:45.120
books out in the world and
I was like, okay, amazing.

00:43:45.120 --> 00:43:47.490
I can be myself I can be
trying to be a good person.

00:43:47.490 --> 00:43:50.440
And I can also be a better leader,
a better entrepreneur in doing so.

00:43:50.440 --> 00:43:54.519
And I have a consistent theoretical
base and evidence base behind this and

00:43:54.519 --> 00:43:56.110
I found that super helpful.

00:43:57.210 --> 00:44:01.675
&gt;&gt; There's a lot on empathetic leadership
for sure that I that I've heard yeah.

00:44:01.675 --> 00:44:05.749
Here's another question from the audience
of how did you balance your academic

00:44:05.749 --> 00:44:09.105
schedule with all the other
opportunities available at the GSB?

00:44:09.105 --> 00:44:14.022
And what is the typical week in
the life of a student look like?

00:44:14.022 --> 00:44:15.940
Fiona smiling,
do you want to take that one?

00:44:15.940 --> 00:44:19.677
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I'm laughing because when I went

00:44:19.677 --> 00:44:23.250
into consulting which is famously
long hours profession but

00:44:23.250 --> 00:44:27.460
I definitely have more free
time now than I did at the GSB.

00:44:27.460 --> 00:44:31.840
Just because I scheduled everything so
tightly at the GSB right.

00:44:31.840 --> 00:44:36.030
It wasn't that I was doing academics for
60 hours a week.

00:44:36.030 --> 00:44:40.034
But I was doing something from
8AM to midnight every day, and

00:44:40.034 --> 00:44:43.070
it's just a calendar management game.

00:44:43.070 --> 00:44:46.875
You quickly decide how much effort
you want to put into academics and

00:44:46.875 --> 00:44:48.820
people vary a ton on that, right?

00:44:48.820 --> 00:44:53.877
You want to put in absolutely enough to
get value out of your classes, but you can

00:44:53.877 --> 00:44:58.876
push very far past that or you can do
the bare minimum to be learning something.

00:44:58.876 --> 00:45:03.360
And so that determines how many hours
you need to devote to academics.

00:45:03.360 --> 00:45:08.346
And the rest of it will fill up with
everything from coffee chats with

00:45:08.346 --> 00:45:13.428
people to club events to phone,
which is our Tuesday night parties.

00:45:13.428 --> 00:45:15.457
And as long as you keep a good calendar,

00:45:15.457 --> 00:45:18.050
it's incredible how much
you can fit into a week.

00:45:19.980 --> 00:45:25.189
I think to add on to that,
the framework that I often give people

00:45:25.189 --> 00:45:30.920
is first start with like,
what are you doing all of this for?

00:45:30.920 --> 00:45:35.784
Like if you have to pick three to five
priorities that you're going to use for

00:45:35.784 --> 00:45:39.570
as a decision rubric for
how you spend your time.

00:45:39.570 --> 00:45:41.430
What are you doing all of this for?

00:45:41.430 --> 00:45:46.418
And having clarity on that, I think makes
everything else much easier because

00:45:46.418 --> 00:45:50.951
then you can say, okay, this quarter
this month, this week this day,

00:45:50.951 --> 00:45:55.160
I have 20 options,
which of those serve that purpose?

00:45:55.160 --> 00:45:58.180
And then how am I going
to mix them together to.

00:45:58.180 --> 00:46:01.610
Make this all work and
the reality is like,

00:46:01.610 --> 00:46:06.868
if you don't use calendars you're
going to have a rough two years.

00:46:06.868 --> 00:46:12.051
[LAUGH] And I think that you have to
decide where do you need to double

00:46:12.051 --> 00:46:17.470
down and spend time,
I took classes where I learned a lot.

00:46:17.470 --> 00:46:22.830
But I didn't necessarily spend a lot of
hours either because of my background or

00:46:22.830 --> 00:46:27.630
because the content was structured
such that I didn't have to do a lot of

00:46:27.630 --> 00:46:31.760
stuff outside class based
done the course itself.

00:46:31.760 --> 00:46:35.200
And so you can kind of mix and
match those, right?

00:46:35.200 --> 00:46:40.114
Like it may not be a good idea in
the quarter that you're trying to plan

00:46:40.114 --> 00:46:45.283
a conference, and also recruit,
to take a bunch of classes that require

00:46:45.283 --> 00:46:50.170
you to do four hours of case reading and
writing before each class.

00:46:50.170 --> 00:46:54.340
So you Kind of just figure that out for
yourself.

00:46:54.340 --> 00:46:59.796
And the last thing I would say is think
about kind of how things build on each

00:46:59.796 --> 00:47:05.705
other as you're determining your
courses and in other our experiences.

00:47:05.705 --> 00:47:09.514
So for example, I wanted to take
certain classes before I went and

00:47:09.514 --> 00:47:11.605
did my internship abroad.

00:47:11.605 --> 00:47:15.178
Because I wanted to take some
of those perspectives and

00:47:15.178 --> 00:47:18.758
go see what it looked like
when I was like on the ground.

00:47:18.758 --> 00:47:22.696
And so those are some subtle things
I would also just add to think about.

00:47:25.799 --> 00:47:30.442
&gt;&gt; I see a question here touching concrete
disclusure that was brought briefly

00:47:30.442 --> 00:47:34.937
before and how it aid to that
clubitive culture and learning by but

00:47:34.937 --> 00:47:38.366
this question really
another that talk about.

00:47:38.366 --> 00:47:42.096
How does that actually push you
to take some academic risks and

00:47:42.096 --> 00:47:47.340
to try some classes that might be really
hard and outside of your comfort zone?

00:47:47.340 --> 00:47:51.142
Can any of you share an example where
maybe you took a class that was a little

00:47:51.142 --> 00:47:54.104
bit scary or intimidating or
outside your comfort zone?

00:48:00.147 --> 00:48:04.001
&gt;&gt; I was really scared to take financial
trading strategies which ended

00:48:04.001 --> 00:48:06.480
up being one of my favorite classes.

00:48:06.480 --> 00:48:12.007
Because it's a class where you spend
a bunch of your time in class and a large

00:48:12.007 --> 00:48:17.824
portion of your grade is determined by
how you do in these market simulations.

00:48:17.824 --> 00:48:23.036
Where you are trading stocks or
options or whatever the topic of that

00:48:23.036 --> 00:48:29.176
class period is, in real time in a sort
of compressed simulation of the market.

00:48:29.176 --> 00:48:34.207
And I came to the GSB from
zero finance background.

00:48:34.207 --> 00:48:41.180
I had really only taken my required first
year in GSB finance courses before this.

00:48:41.180 --> 00:48:44.520
So it was kind of a leap of
faith to take that class but

00:48:44.520 --> 00:48:49.496
it ended up being one of the coolest
classes I took, because it really taught

00:48:49.496 --> 00:48:53.618
me how the market actually works and
what options really are and

00:48:53.618 --> 00:48:58.318
what they mean and how people trade
them beyond the academic side of it.

00:48:58.318 --> 00:49:00.900
But I had learned in my
foundation finance class.

00:49:03.470 --> 00:49:08.118
&gt;&gt; I think I have a similar story to Ilana
where I also have no finance coming into

00:49:08.118 --> 00:49:08.790
the GSB.

00:49:08.790 --> 00:49:11.380
In fact, at one of these admin receptions,

00:49:11.380 --> 00:49:15.790
I met a fellow classmate who told me they
were in PE and I asked her if she was in

00:49:15.790 --> 00:49:19.997
physical education because I had no
idea that private equity was a job.

00:49:19.997 --> 00:49:25.113
And I ended up taking like over seven
different finance classes at the GSB.

00:49:25.113 --> 00:49:26.810
Most of them I took pass fail in the end.

00:49:26.810 --> 00:49:33.450
So corporate financial modeling,
the financial restructuring debt class.

00:49:33.450 --> 00:49:37.429
These are all things that have
absolutely zero background in and

00:49:37.429 --> 00:49:42.526
I think because of great on disclosure and
generous pass fail policies at the GSB.

00:49:42.526 --> 00:49:46.536
I was able to take these classes that I
was interested in knowing that I had no

00:49:46.536 --> 00:49:50.610
background in them but wanted to learn
more about it and was able to now having

00:49:50.610 --> 00:49:54.580
graduated have a decent amount of
finance knowledge relative to before.

00:49:57.850 --> 00:50:01.061
&gt;&gt; This next question maybe
Diego I'll have you kick us off here.

00:50:01.061 --> 00:50:04.842
But what advice would you have for
incoming MBA students who are really

00:50:04.842 --> 00:50:07.536
trying to maximize their
academic experience and

00:50:07.536 --> 00:50:10.050
their their classroom
experience at the GSB?

00:50:10.050 --> 00:50:11.580
What advice would you give people?

00:50:14.184 --> 00:50:17.452
I'll take the focus advice.

00:50:17.452 --> 00:50:21.415
If someone else has the go explore advice,
go for it.

00:50:21.415 --> 00:50:23.966
[LAUGH] But
I came in laser focused, right.

00:50:23.966 --> 00:50:28.586
My essay was about Lola,
it talked about, like by name,

00:50:28.586 --> 00:50:31.280
this is the thing I want to build.

00:50:31.280 --> 00:50:35.587
So that helped me cut through
a lot of that noise, there's so

00:50:35.587 --> 00:50:38.552
many opportunities that you could take.

00:50:38.552 --> 00:50:41.930
It helped me say no to a lot of things.

00:50:41.930 --> 00:50:47.622
Really focus on what classes
to take every single project

00:50:47.622 --> 00:50:54.629
in every single class somehow was
geared towards this organization.

00:50:54.629 --> 00:50:59.346
A lot of classmates used me and
Lola to dip their toes into social

00:50:59.346 --> 00:51:03.090
entrepreneurship, into sub garage,
and so on.

00:51:03.090 --> 00:51:04.600
So that was beautiful.

00:51:05.980 --> 00:51:09.877
Many of the relationships that I
ended up building with professors,

00:51:09.877 --> 00:51:13.706
with staff from the Center for
Social Innovation, with classmates

00:51:13.706 --> 00:51:16.144
have all been hugely valuable
&gt;&gt; And

00:51:16.144 --> 00:51:21.350
the others want to share advice on how
to maximize your academic experience.

00:51:25.100 --> 00:51:29.193
&gt;&gt; I think you should,
I came in with not one thing but

00:51:29.193 --> 00:51:35.130
like three things that I was really
gearing my academics around.

00:51:35.130 --> 00:51:42.310
One was developing economies the other
was, interpersonal development/leadership.

00:51:42.310 --> 00:51:46.682
And then the third one was strategy.

00:51:46.682 --> 00:51:51.103
And so I think for me as I thought
about what classes to take,

00:51:51.103 --> 00:51:53.588
they had to revolve around that.

00:51:53.588 --> 00:51:58.295
And you'll notice I didn't say
entrepreneurship anywhere in

00:51:58.295 --> 00:52:03.354
there which confuses people because
I managed to get out of the GSB,

00:52:03.354 --> 00:52:08.883
taking only one entrepreneurship
[LAUGH] managing growing enterprises.

00:52:08.883 --> 00:52:14.022
And I think that like a healthy
amount of curiosity is important but

00:52:14.022 --> 00:52:19.712
that looks different for everyone and
my biggest piece of advice is like,

00:52:19.712 --> 00:52:23.108
don't try to replicate
what alumnus did and

00:52:23.108 --> 00:52:27.548
don't try to replicate what
your classmates are doing.

00:52:27.548 --> 00:52:32.490
Because even if you do exactly what they
did, you'll come out with a completely

00:52:32.490 --> 00:52:37.384
different experience anyway, so you
might as well live your own experience.

00:52:37.384 --> 00:52:40.802
And I think that just
takes some courage to be,

00:52:40.802 --> 00:52:45.973
this is what I think I want to do and
whatever that looks just go with it and

00:52:45.973 --> 00:52:50.462
the luxury is every quarter you
can kind of change your mind.

00:52:50.462 --> 00:52:52.685
But you only get six of them.

00:52:52.685 --> 00:52:55.165
So, yeah, make use of them.

00:52:56.725 --> 00:52:59.065
And so that's how I think about it.

00:52:59.065 --> 00:53:00.929
&gt;&gt; Great advice.

00:53:00.929 --> 00:53:02.655
Well, I want to be cognizant of my time.

00:53:02.655 --> 00:53:07.739
So I want to thank all of our panelists
today for sharing your perspectives.

00:53:07.739 --> 00:53:12.660
I also hope that this is just the start
of our attendees MBA journey.

00:53:12.660 --> 00:53:16.704
And one way to continue that
is through my stanford.mba.

00:53:16.704 --> 00:53:19.690
If you haven't already been to the site,
definitely check it out.

00:53:19.690 --> 00:53:21.990
You can create your own
personalized dashboard.

00:53:21.990 --> 00:53:27.185
That'll tee up classes or clubs that
might be of interest to you students or

00:53:27.185 --> 00:53:30.159
alumni profiles that you might relate to.

00:53:30.159 --> 00:53:33.607
If you start the application, you can
also track your progress through that and

00:53:33.607 --> 00:53:35.525
learn about other great events like today.

00:53:35.525 --> 00:53:38.074
So again, thank you so
much for joining us.

00:53:38.074 --> 00:53:42.735
We really appreciate it and best of
luck on your MBA journey, thank you

00:53:42.735 --> 00:53:47.643
